<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Banu Khurasan</h1><p class="st-meta">1059 CE – 1159 CE · capital Tunis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A local dynasty that ruled Tunis as an independent city-state after the Zirid collapse, until the Almohad conquest.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hilal">Banu Hilal</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zirid-emirate">Zirid emirate</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a> was abandoned in 1057, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> broke away. Around 1059 its notables chose Abd al-Haqq ibn Abd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-aziz">al-Aziz</a> ibn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khurasan</a>, from a local family of notables, as ruler, founding the Khurasanid dynasty in the old port city.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Khurasanids ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> and its hinterland as a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> city-state, at first recognising the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hammadid-emirate">Hammadid emirate</a> as overlord to secure protection from the Arab tribes and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zirid-dynasty">Zirids of Mahdia</a>. They paid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> for peace, strengthened the walls and encouraged trade by sea. Ahmad ibn Abd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-aziz">al-Aziz</a>, the most notable ruler, built palaces and gathered scholars at his court.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The dynasty was briefly displaced by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hammadid-emirate">Hammadids</a> in 1128, but returned. In 1159 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-mumin">Abd al-Mu'min</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>, ending its independence, and the city later became the capital of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsids</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/banu-khurasan.jpg" alt="Banu Khurasan" width="320" height="396"><figcaption>Image: Meyer's Handatlas · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carte_Tunisie_1844.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1059 CE – 1159 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tunis</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abd al-Haqq ibn Khurasan, Ahmad ibn Abd al-Aziz</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Khurasanids, Emirate of Tunis</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1109&amp;focus=banu-khurasan" aria-label="Banu Khurasan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4935 851 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Banu Khurasan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5255 1107L5255 1145M5255 1107L5121 1140M5255 1107L4794 1282"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5255 1145h0M5121 1140h0M4794 1282h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5255 1107h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5255 1107h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tunis. <a href="/atlas?year=1109&amp;focus=banu-khurasan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
